From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:42:17 +0900
Subject: Follow aliases in an inline call's arguments before exposing opaque
 types

When an inline method defined beside an opaque type is called, the inliner
looks through each value argument's type for references to the object that
owns the opaque type, and casts the argument to the same type seen through a
proxy that exposes the alias, so that the inlined body can use the argument as
its underlying type. It looked at the widened, dealiased type, which follows
aliases only at the top of the type. An opaque type named through an alias
anywhere else was not found, the argument was not cast, and the body was typed
against the still-opaque type: "expression does not take parameters", or a
mismatch against the underlying type.

Two kinds of alias were missed.

An applied match alias is not dealiased at all: for

    type Cell[value] = value match
      case Int => Cell.Whole
      case _   => Cell.Ref[value]

an argument of type `Cell[String]` mentions neither `Cell.Ref` nor the object
`Cell`. Typer hides this, because it simplifies the type of every tree it
types, the declared type of a definition included, and simplifying reduces
match aliases; a definition read from TASTy keeps its type as written. So the
call failed whenever the argument was a member of a separately compiled class
declared with such a type, and for every such call in a unit recompiled with
`-from-tasty`.

An ordinary alias below the top is left alone: with `export Ops.O`, an
argument of type `O { type Form = Int }` or `List[O]` names the opaque type
`Ops.O` only through the alias the export made, in a single compilation too.

The argument's type now has its aliases followed at any depth, match aliases
reduced where they can be, before the opaque references are sought in it.

The following is confined to what upstream missed, in two ways. It is done only
for an argument whose type, as it stands, mentions no object with opaque
members: one that does is cast exactly as upstream casts it, so that with
`type Bytes = Frozen[Byte]` a `Frozen[Bytes]` keeps `Bytes` as its element type
and is not given `Array[Byte]` elements, which separation checking rejects
where the caller expects a `Frozen[Byte]` back. And it stops at an opaque type
even where that is an alias, as it is through the opaque proxy of an enclosing
inline call: a lambda over `$proxy1.Scribe[Char]`, passed on to a second inline
method, was otherwise cast to a function of the representation, and could no
longer be applied to an opaque value made in the inlined body.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code); reviewed content.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: matchopaque
